{{Politics of Balisca}}
In Balisca, the term '''autonomous commune''' ({{lang-bl|comuna autònoma}}) refers to {{W|intentional community|intentional communities}} within [[communes of Balisca|communes]], consisting of neighborhoods or districts in Baliscan cities that functions as even lower administrative divisions. Recognition of these self-described communes was ensured with the passage of the [[Constitution of Balisca|Constitution of 2022]], which authorized their existence with the [[Thirty-sixth Amendment to the Baliscan Constitution|36th Amendment]]. As of August 2022, there are only only three existent autonomous communes:  [[Capitoline Hill]] ([[Castejón]]), [[El Pobleno]] ([[Concepción]]), and [[tba]] (tba); although there are more than twenty localities currently in the process of transitioning to autonomous communes.

The governance of the autonomous communes is deeply rooted in the ideas of {{W|open-source governance}}, {{W|radical democracy}}, {{W|social anarchism}} and {{W|digital socialism}} making the communes collectively the largest territories since the [[W:Spanish Revolution of 1936|Spanish Revolution of 1936]] to be governed as such, and some of the world's few societies to be fully under some form of anarchist influence. These communes additionally operate on the ideals of {{W|mutual aid}}, {{W|worker self-management}}, and widespread {{W|cooperatives}}. The proponents of the these autonomous communes state that they are the first true {{W|direct democracy|direct democracies}}, and are efficiently taking on an {{W|Anarchism|anarchistic}}, {{W|Feminism|feminist}}, and {{W|libertarian socialist}} ideology to promote {{W|decentralization}}, {{W|gender equality}}, {{W|environmental sustainability}}, {{W|social ecology}} and {{W|just society|pluralistic tolerance for religious, cultural and political diversity}}. 

With the advent of {{W|social media}}, {{W|e-democracy}}, {{W|open-source governance|open-politics}} and the widespread {{W|technological literacy}} of the Baliscan population, it is stated by supporters that the autonomous communes are the next step in the evolution of {{w|direct democracy}}, and demonstrate that the {{W|anti-statism|Baliscan state is becoming increasingly redundant}}.

